-- Migration: Bloom's L3 rebalancing — TCP batch 1 (20 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-21
-- Purpose: Rewrite 20 L2 (Application) questions to L3 (Analysis) depth for TCP section
-- Affected topics: Entity Formation and Liquidation, Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules, International Tax, Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies, Retirement Plans and Education Savings, Trusts and Estates, Owner-Entity Transactions, Nontaxable Dispositions, Individual Tax Planning: Compensation, Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns

BEGIN;

-- ============================================================
-- ENTITY FORMATION AND LIQUIDATION (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1022: Entity Formation and Liquidation — Effect of boot on Section 351 nonrecognition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Technologies is being formed by three founders. Founder A contributes equipment (basis $45,000, FMV $120,000) for 40% of the stock. Founder B contributes a patent (basis $10,000, FMV $120,000) for 40% of the stock. Founder C contributes $120,000 cash for 20% of the stock. Founder A also receives $25,000 cash from the corporation. How does the receipt of boot by Founder A affect the nonrecognition analysis differently for Founder A compared to Founder B, and what is the tax consequence?',
  choices = '["Both founders are treated identically because all transferors in a Section 351 exchange receive the same tax treatment regardless of whether boot is received", "Founder A must recognize the entire $75,000 realized gain because receiving boot disqualifies A from Section 351 treatment entirely, while Founder B retains nonrecognition", "Founder A recognizes no gain because the boot is treated as a loan repayment, and Founder B recognizes $110,000 of gain because patents are not qualifying property under Section 351", "Founder A must recognize $25,000 of gain because boot received triggers recognition up to the lesser of boot received or realized gain — since A''s realized gain of $75,000 exceeds the $25,000 boot, A recognizes $25,000, while Founder B recognizes no gain because B received no boot despite having a larger built-in gain of $110,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under IRC Section 351(b), when a transferor receives boot (non-stock consideration) in an otherwise qualifying Section 351 exchange, gain is recognized to the extent of the lesser of (1) the boot received or (2) the realized gain. Founder A has a realized gain of $75,000 ($120,000 FMV minus $45,000 basis) and received $25,000 boot, so A recognizes $25,000. Founder B has a larger realized gain of $110,000 ($120,000 minus $10,000) but received no boot, so B recognizes nothing — the built-in gain remains deferred in B''s stock basis. This illustrates a critical analytical point: the amount of built-in gain is irrelevant absent boot; only the interaction between boot received and realized gain triggers recognition. (A) is wrong because Section 351(b) treats each transferor individually based on their own boot received. (B) is wrong because boot does not disqualify the exchange — it merely triggers partial recognition. (C) is wrong because patents are qualifying property under Section 351.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 1022;

-- ID 1023: Entity Formation and Liquidation — Analyzing services contribution on control requirement
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Consulting is being incorporated. Partner A contributes software (basis $30,000, FMV $200,000) for 60% of the stock. Partner B contributes consulting services valued at $100,000 for 30% of the stock. Partner C contributes $50,000 cash for 10% of the stock. How does Partner B''s services contribution affect the Section 351 control analysis for the entire group, and what are the tax consequences for each participant?',
  choices = '["All three participants qualify for Section 351 nonrecognition because the group collectively owns 100% of the stock immediately after the exchange", "Partner B''s shares are excluded from the 80% control test because services are not ''property'' under Section 351 — but A and C together hold 70%, which fails the 80% control threshold, so all participants must recognize their gains in full", "Partner B''s services contribution is excluded from the control test, but since A and C together contribute property and hold 70%, they fail the 80% control requirement — however, B would still be taxed on the $100,000 compensation regardless of whether Section 351 applies to A and C", "Partner B''s shares count toward the 80% control test because B also contributed nominal property alongside services, satisfying the ''property transferor'' requirement for all participants"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 351(a), nonrecognition applies only when transferors of ''property'' control (80% or more under Section 368(c)) the corporation immediately after the exchange. Services are explicitly excluded from the definition of property under Section 351(d)(1). Partner B''s 30% is excluded from the control calculation because B contributed only services. Partners A (60%) and C (10%) are the only property transferors, holding just 70% — below the 80% threshold. Consequently, Section 351 fails for the entire exchange: A must recognize the $170,000 gain ($200,000 FMV minus $30,000 basis) and B must recognize $100,000 of compensation income. This analysis demonstrates how one participant''s non-property contribution can disqualify the entire group. (A) is wrong because it ignores the services exclusion. (C) correctly identifies the 70% failure but incorrectly separates B''s tax treatment from the Section 351 analysis — B is taxed on compensation income regardless. (D) is wrong because B contributed no property.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1023;

-- ============================================================
-- PASSIVE ACTIVITY AND AT-RISK RULES (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 910: Passive Activity — Effect of netting passive income and losses across activities
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dr. Waverly, a physician with AGI of $180,000, owns interests in three passive activities. Activity A (rental apartment complex) generates $60,000 of passive income. Activity B (limited partnership restaurant) generates $85,000 of passive losses. Activity C (rental storage facility) generates $15,000 of passive losses. Dr. Waverly actively participates in both rental activities but not the restaurant. How does the netting of these passive activities affect her taxable income, and why does the $25,000 rental loss allowance not apply?',
  choices = '["Dr. Waverly can deduct the full $100,000 of passive losses because the $60,000 passive income offsets part of the losses and the $25,000 active participation allowance covers the remaining $40,000", "Dr. Waverly nets all three activities: $60,000 income minus $100,000 losses produces a net passive loss of $40,000, of which $0 is deductible — the $25,000 rental allowance is unavailable because her AGI exceeds $150,000, where the allowance is fully phased out, so the entire $40,000 is suspended", "Dr. Waverly can deduct $25,000 of the net $40,000 passive loss because she actively participates in the rental activities, and the remaining $15,000 is suspended", "Dr. Waverly must keep rental and nonrental passive activities in separate baskets — the $60,000 rental income cannot offset the $85,000 restaurant loss, resulting in $25,000 suspended rental losses and $85,000 suspended nonrental losses"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 469, all passive activities are netted together regardless of type (rental vs. nonrental). The $60,000 passive income offsets $60,000 of the combined $100,000 passive losses, leaving a net passive loss of $40,000. The Section 469(i) $25,000 active participation rental allowance begins phasing out at AGI of $100,000 and is completely eliminated at $150,000 (50% of the excess over $100,000). Since Dr. Waverly''s AGI is $180,000, the allowance is fully phased out ($180,000 - $100,000 = $80,000 excess; 50% x $80,000 = $40,000 reduction, exceeding the $25,000 allowance). Therefore, the entire $40,000 net passive loss is suspended and carried forward. (A) is wrong because the rental allowance is phased out at her income level. (C) ignores the AGI phase-out. (D) is wrong because passive activities are netted in a single basket, not separated by rental vs. nonrental.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 910;

-- ID 911: Passive Activity — Analyzing real estate professional status effect on loss characterization
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Marcus Chen is a licensed real estate broker who spent 1,200 hours in real property trades or businesses and 900 hours in his brokerage during 2026. His wife Elena is an emergency room physician who spent 2,400 hours in her medical practice. They file jointly. Marcus owns a rental property that generated a $75,000 loss, and he spent 600 hours managing it. Elena has $350,000 in W-2 income. How does Marcus''s real estate professional status under Section 469(c)(7) affect the treatment of the rental loss, and what would change if Marcus had only spent 500 hours in real property activities?',
  choices = '["Marcus qualifies as a real estate professional because his 1,200 hours exceed both the 750-hour threshold and more than half of his personal services, allowing the $75,000 rental loss to be treated as nonpassive and fully deductible against Elena''s W-2 income — if Marcus had only 500 hours, the rental loss would revert to passive and be limited to $25,000 (subject to AGI phase-out)", "Marcus does not qualify as a real estate professional because the couple''s combined hours in real estate (1,200) are less than their combined hours in non-real-estate activities (2,400), and the $75,000 loss is fully suspended", "Marcus qualifies as a real estate professional, but the $75,000 rental loss is still limited to the $25,000 active participation allowance because the real estate professional exception only removes the per se passive characterization for commercial properties, not residential rentals", "Marcus cannot qualify because real estate professional status requires both spouses to independently meet the 750-hour test when filing jointly"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 469(c)(7), a taxpayer qualifies as a real estate professional if (1) more than half of personal services performed during the year are in real property trades or businesses, and (2) the taxpayer performs more than 750 hours in real property trades or businesses. These tests are applied individually, not jointly — only one spouse must qualify. Marcus''s 1,200 hours exceed 750, and those 1,200 hours exceed half of his total personal service hours (1,200 + 900 = 2,100 total; 1,200/2,100 = 57%). His 600 hours of material participation in the rental activity satisfies the material participation test. The rental loss is therefore recharacterized as nonpassive and deductible against Elena''s ordinary income without limitation. If Marcus had only 500 hours, he would fail the 750-hour threshold, the rental loss would be passive, and at their AGI level ($350,000+), the $25,000 allowance is fully phased out — the entire loss would be suspended. (B) is wrong because the test is individual, not joint. (C) is wrong because the exception applies to all rental real estate, not just commercial. (D) is wrong because only one spouse must qualify.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 911;

