-- INTERNATIONAL TAX (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 966: International Tax — Analyzing Subpart F income vs GILTI inclusion differences
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Corp, a U.S. parent, owns 100% of two controlled foreign corporations. CFC-Alpha earns $2,000,000 from passive investments (interest and dividends from unrelated parties) and $500,000 from manufacturing. CFC-Beta earns $3,000,000 entirely from active manufacturing with $12,000,000 of qualified business asset investment (QBAI). How do the Subpart F rules and the GILTI regime treat these two CFCs differently, and why does CFC-Alpha''s income trigger a more immediate and complete U.S. tax consequence than CFC-Beta''s?',
  choices = '["Both CFCs are treated identically under Subpart F because all CFC income is included in the U.S. shareholder''s income regardless of the income''s character or source", "CFC-Alpha''s passive income avoids Subpart F because the de minimis exception applies when passive income is less than 5% of gross income, while CFC-Beta''s active income is fully taxed under GILTI", "CFC-Alpha''s $2,000,000 of passive income is Subpart F income included in full under Section 951(a) with no QBAI offset, while CFC-Beta''s $3,000,000 manufacturing income enters the GILTI computation under Section 951A where the 10% deemed return on $12,000,000 QBAI ($1,200,000) reduces the net GILTI inclusion to $1,800,000 — Subpart F income is more punitive because it allows no tangible asset offset", "CFC-Alpha and CFC-Beta are both subject to GILTI only, because the TCJA eliminated the Subpart F regime for all CFC income earned after 2017"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 951(a), Subpart F income — which includes foreign personal holding company income such as passive interest and dividends per Section 954(c) — is included dollar-for-dollar in the U.S. shareholder''s income with no offset for tangible assets. CFC-Alpha''s $2,000,000 of passive income is fully included. In contrast, CFC-Beta''s $3,000,000 of active manufacturing income is ''tested income'' under the GILTI regime (Section 951A). GILTI allows a deemed tangible income return equal to 10% of QBAI ($12,000,000 x 10% = $1,200,000), reducing the net GILTI inclusion to $1,800,000. Additionally, corporate U.S. shareholders receive a 50% Section 250 deduction on GILTI, effectively halving the tax rate. Subpart F income does not receive the Section 250 deduction or the QBAI offset, making it the more punitive regime. (A) is wrong because Subpart F and GILTI have different mechanics. (B) is wrong because the de minimis exception requires passive income below the lesser of 5% of gross income or $1 million. (D) is wrong because Subpart F remains in effect post-TCJA.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 966;

-- ID 967: International Tax — Analyzing GILTI deemed tangible income return effect on inclusion
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Bancroft Industries, a domestic C corporation, owns 100% of two CFCs. CFC-1 is a software company with $4,000,000 of tested income and $2,000,000 of QBAI. CFC-2 is a capital-intensive manufacturer with $2,000,000 of tested income and $25,000,000 of QBAI. How does the QBAI difference between the two CFCs affect Bancroft''s aggregate GILTI inclusion, and what does this reveal about the policy rationale behind the deemed tangible income return?',
  choices = '["Bancroft calculates GILTI separately for each CFC — CFC-1 produces a $3,800,000 GILTI inclusion and CFC-2 produces zero — and the two results cannot be combined because GILTI is computed on a per-entity basis", "Bancroft''s GILTI inclusion is $6,000,000 because the QBAI offset only applies to tested income from manufacturing activities, and software income is excluded from the QBAI computation", "Bancroft''s GILTI inclusion is zero because total QBAI of $27,000,000 produces a deemed return ($2,700,000) that exceeds the software company''s tested income, eliminating the entire inclusion for both CFCs", "Bancroft aggregates both CFCs: total tested income of $6,000,000 minus a deemed tangible return of $2,700,000 (10% of $27,000,000 combined QBAI) yields a net GILTI inclusion of $3,300,000 — the high-QBAI manufacturer effectively shields $2,500,000 of its own income plus $200,000 of the software company''s income, revealing that GILTI is designed to tax excess returns above a routine return on tangible assets"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under IRC Section 951A, GILTI is computed at the U.S. shareholder level by aggregating all CFCs. Total tested income is $6,000,000 ($4,000,000 + $2,000,000). The deemed tangible income return (DTIR) is 10% of aggregate QBAI: 10% x ($2,000,000 + $25,000,000) = $2,700,000. Net GILTI inclusion equals $6,000,000 - $2,700,000 = $3,300,000. The aggregation mechanism is significant: CFC-2''s excess QBAI effectively shields not only its own $2,000,000 of income but also reduces the inclusion attributable to the asset-light software company. This reveals the policy design — GILTI targets ''excess'' intangible returns (returns above a routine 10% return on tangible capital), which is why asset-heavy businesses generate less GILTI exposure. Bancroft also receives a 50% Section 250 deduction on the $3,300,000 inclusion. (A) is wrong because GILTI is computed at the shareholder level, not per-CFC. (B) is wrong because QBAI applies to all tested income regardless of activity type. (C) is wrong because the DTIR is subtracted from total tested income, not compared to individual CFC amounts.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 967;

