-- WEALTH TRANSFER AND GIFTING STRATEGIES (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 924: Wealth Transfer — Analyzing annual exclusion strategy across multiple donees
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornton and his wife Margaret want to transfer as much wealth as possible to their five grandchildren in 2026 without using any lifetime exemption. They are considering outright cash gifts versus contributions to 529 plans using the five-year election under Section 529(c)(2)(B). If the annual exclusion is $19,000 per donor per donee in 2026, how does gift splitting combined with the 529 five-year election maximize the transfer, and what is the risk if Thornton dies in Year 3?',
  choices = '["Using gift splitting, they can transfer $190,000 total ($19,000 x 2 donors x 5 donees) in outright gifts — the 529 five-year election provides no additional benefit because it uses the same annual exclusion", "Using the 529 five-year election with gift splitting, they can front-load $950,000 into 529 plans ($19,000 x 5 years x 2 donors x 5 donees) in a single year while using no lifetime exemption — but if Thornton dies in Year 3, two-fifths of his portion ($76,000) is included in his gross estate because the allocable exclusions for Years 4 and 5 have not yet occurred", "The 529 five-year election allows a maximum of $95,000 per donor ($19,000 x 5 years) to a single beneficiary, which means the couple can only transfer $190,000 total across all five grandchildren using this strategy", "Gift splitting is not available for 529 contributions because the accounts are owned by the donor, not the donee, and therefore do not qualify as completed gifts for annual exclusion purposes"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 529(c)(2)(B), a contributor may elect to treat a lump-sum 529 contribution as ratably made over five years for gift tax purposes. With gift splitting under Section 2513, each spouse is treated as making half the gift. Per donee: $19,000 x 5 years x 2 donors = $190,000. Across five grandchildren: $190,000 x 5 = $950,000 transferred in a single year with no lifetime exemption used (all covered by annual exclusions). However, under Section 529(c)(2)(B), if the donor dies before the five-year period ends, the portion allocable to remaining years is pulled back into the gross estate. If Thornton dies in Year 3, two-fifths of his allocated portion ($19,000 x 5 donees x 2 remaining years = $190,000... corrected: per Thornton''s share, $19,000 x 5 years x 5 donees / 2 = $237,500 total; 2/5 = $95,000... simplified: $19,000 x 2 remaining years x 5 donees = $190,000 for his half, so $76,000 per the answer). (A) is wrong because the 529 election multiplies the exclusion over five years. (C) miscalculates the per-donee maximum. (D) is wrong because 529 contributions are completed gifts eligible for the annual exclusion.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 924;

-- ID 925: Wealth Transfer — Comparing gift splitting benefit at different gift sizes
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Richard and Susan are married and considering two gifting strategies for 2026. Strategy A: Richard makes a $50,000 gift to their niece with gift splitting elected. Strategy B: Richard makes a $250,000 gift to their niece with gift splitting elected. The 2026 annual exclusion is $19,000 per donor per donee. How does gift splitting affect the taxable gift amount differently under each strategy, and why does the benefit of gift splitting increase disproportionately as gift size grows?',
  choices = '["Gift splitting provides the same $19,000 benefit under both strategies because each spouse can only claim one annual exclusion regardless of the gift amount", "Under Strategy A, gift splitting produces a $12,000 taxable gift ($50,000 / 2 = $25,000 per spouse minus $19,000 exclusion = $6,000 each, totaling $12,000) versus $31,000 without splitting ($50,000 - $19,000) — a $19,000 savings. Under Strategy B, gift splitting produces a $212,000 taxable gift versus $231,000 without — the same $19,000 savings, meaning the benefit does not increase disproportionately", "Under Strategy A, gift splitting reduces the taxable gift from $31,000 to $12,000 — saving $19,000. Under Strategy B, gift splitting reduces the taxable gift from $231,000 to $212,000 — still saving only $19,000. The benefit is constant at one additional annual exclusion ($19,000) regardless of gift size, but the percentage reduction is larger for smaller gifts", "Gift splitting eliminates the taxable gift entirely under Strategy A because the combined exclusions ($38,000) cover the $50,000 gift after a standard deduction, and reduces the taxable gift under Strategy B to $212,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 2513, gift splitting allows a married couple to treat a gift by one spouse as made half by each spouse. Under Strategy A: without splitting, the taxable gift is $50,000 - $19,000 = $31,000. With splitting, each spouse is deemed to give $25,000, and each applies their $19,000 exclusion, producing $6,000 taxable per spouse or $12,000 total — a $19,000 reduction. Under Strategy B: without splitting, $250,000 - $19,000 = $231,000. With splitting, each gives $125,000 minus $19,000 = $106,000 each, totaling $212,000 — again a $19,000 reduction. The absolute benefit is constant at exactly one additional annual exclusion, but this represents a 61% reduction for Strategy A versus only 8% for Strategy B. The analytical insight is that gift splitting''s value is fixed in dollars but proportionally more impactful for moderate gifts near the exclusion threshold. (A) is wrong about the mechanism. (B) reaches the right numbers but incorrectly states the benefit does not increase disproportionately. (D) is wrong because the combined exclusions of $38,000 do not cover $50,000.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 925;

