-- ID 938: Retirement Plans — Analyzing 401(k) vs Roth 401(k) tax consequences over time
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greenleaf Industries offers both traditional and Roth 401(k) options. Employee Diane Chen, age 35 and in the 24% bracket, is deciding between contributing $23,500 (the 2026 maximum for under-50) to either option. She expects to be in the 32% bracket at retirement in 30 years. Assuming both accounts earn identical 7% annual returns, how does the choice between traditional and Roth 401(k) affect her after-tax retirement wealth, and what factor drives the difference?',
  choices = '["The traditional 401(k) produces more after-tax wealth because the upfront tax deduction allows more money to compound over 30 years, and the larger account balance more than offsets taxes at withdrawal", "Both options produce identical after-tax results because the tax benefit of deferral exactly equals the tax cost of withdrawal regardless of the rate differential, as long as contribution amounts are the same", "The Roth 401(k) produces more after-tax wealth because Diane''s expected retirement tax rate (32%) exceeds her current rate (24%) — she pays tax on $23,500 at 24% now ($5,640) rather than paying 32% on the much larger future balance, and the after-tax difference is driven entirely by the rate differential, not the account balance", "The traditional 401(k) is always superior because employer matching contributions are only available in the traditional option, creating a permanent advantage"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The core analytical principle is that when the tax rate at withdrawal differs from the rate at contribution, the Roth and traditional produce different after-tax outcomes. With a traditional 401(k), Diane defers tax at 24% now but pays 32% on all withdrawals including growth. With a Roth, she pays 24% on the $23,500 contribution today and withdraws everything tax-free. If rates were identical, the results would be mathematically equivalent (the ''tax-rate equivalence'' theorem). But because Diane''s future rate exceeds her current rate, every dollar of growth in the traditional account faces an 8-percentage-point penalty at withdrawal that Roth avoids. The driving factor is solely the rate differential — not compounding, account size, or time horizon. (A) is wrong because the larger nominal balance is an illusion; the government effectively owns a larger share of it at the higher rate. (B) is only true when rates are equal. (D) is wrong because employer matches go into the traditional sub-account regardless of the employee''s election.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 938;

-- ID 939: Retirement Plans — Analyzing SEP IRA contribution limits for self-employed vs employees
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dr. Ashworth operates a medical practice as a sole proprietor with net self-employment income of $200,000. She also employs a nurse practitioner with W-2 wages of $120,000. Both are covered by the practice''s SEP IRA. How does the SEP contribution calculation differ for Dr. Ashworth versus her employee, and why does the self-employment tax adjustment reduce the effective contribution rate for the self-employed?',
  choices = '["Dr. Ashworth''s maximum contribution is approximately $37,000 (based on 20% of net self-employment income after the self-employment tax deduction adjustment) while the nurse receives up to $30,000 (25% of $120,000) — the discrepancy arises because self-employed individuals must reduce net earnings by the deductible half of self-employment tax before applying the contribution rate, effectively lowering the base from $200,000 to approximately $185,000", "Both receive contributions of 25% of their respective compensation — $50,000 for Dr. Ashworth and $30,000 for the nurse — because SEP rules apply the same percentage uniformly to all participants", "Dr. Ashworth can contribute more than the nurse because self-employed individuals are allowed a 25% contribution rate plus the employer-equivalent share, producing a total rate of 37.5% of net earnings", "Dr. Ashworth and the nurse both receive identical dollar contributions because SEP plans require equal dollar amounts for all participants under the nondiscrimination rules"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 408(k), SEP IRA contributions for employees are up to 25% of W-2 compensation — the nurse can receive up to $30,000 (25% x $120,000). For self-employed individuals, the calculation under Section 401(d) requires an adjustment: net self-employment income must first be reduced by the deductible half of self-employment tax (approximately $14,130 on $200,000), producing adjusted net earnings of roughly $185,870. The effective contribution rate for the self-employed is 20% (not 25%) because the contribution itself reduces the denominator in a circular calculation. This yields approximately $37,174 for Dr. Ashworth. The self-employment tax adjustment exists because employees have their employer portion paid separately, while the self-employed must account for both halves. The net effect is that self-employed individuals contribute at a lower effective rate on gross income than employees. (B) is wrong because the 25% rate does not apply directly to self-employment income. (C) is wrong because there is no 37.5% rate. (D) is wrong because SEP nondiscrimination requires the same percentage rate, not the same dollar amount.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 939;

