-- ID 1008: Trusts and Estates — Effect of DNI on trust vs beneficiary taxation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Pemberton Family Trust, a complex trust, has $150,000 of gross income ($100,000 taxable interest, $30,000 qualified dividends, $20,000 tax-exempt municipal bond interest) and $10,000 of trustee fees allocable to all income proportionally. The trust distributes $80,000 to Beneficiary A. Trust tax rates reach 37% at approximately $15,200 of taxable income, while Beneficiary A is in the 24% bracket. How does distributable net income (DNI) determine the split of taxation between the trust and the beneficiary, and why does this allocation produce a lower combined tax burden?',
  choices = '["The trust pays tax on all $150,000 of income regardless of distributions because trusts are separate taxable entities, and the beneficiary reports the $80,000 distribution as a nontaxable return of capital", "DNI limits the beneficiary''s inclusion to $80,000, but the trust pays no tax because the distribution fully offsets all trust income through the income distribution deduction", "The trust must distribute all DNI to avoid the 37% rate, and any retained income is subject to the 3.8% net investment income tax in addition to the 37% rate, making retention always disadvantageous", "DNI of approximately $140,000 (gross income minus allocable expenses) caps the trust''s income distribution deduction at $80,000 — the trust deducts $80,000 and pays tax on the remaining $60,000 at compressed trust rates (37%), while the beneficiary reports $80,000 at her 24% rate — the combined burden is lower than if the trust retained all income because shifting $80,000 from the 37% trust bracket to the 24% beneficiary bracket saves approximately $10,400 in total tax"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under IRC Subchapter J (Sections 651-663), DNI serves as both a ceiling on the trust''s income distribution deduction and on the amount taxable to the beneficiary. DNI here is approximately $140,000 ($150,000 gross income minus roughly $10,000 trustee fees, with adjustments for tax-exempt income). The trust claims an income distribution deduction of $80,000 (limited to the taxable portion of DNI, approximately $120,000 after removing tax-exempt income). The trust pays tax on retained taxable income, while the beneficiary reports $80,000 prorated by income character. Because trust rates are severely compressed — reaching 37% at approximately $15,200 — while Beneficiary A is at 24%, shifting income to the beneficiary through distributions materially reduces the combined tax burden. This is the fundamental planning insight of Subchapter J: DNI creates a conduit mechanism that allows income to be taxed at the beneficiary''s potentially lower rate. (A) is wrong because distributions create a deduction-inclusion mechanism. (B) is wrong because $80,000 does not offset all income. (C) is wrong because retention is not always disadvantageous — it depends on the beneficiary''s rate.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 1008;

-- ID 1009: Trusts and Estates — Analyzing character allocation of DNI to beneficiaries
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Hawthorne Trust is a simple trust with DNI of $80,000 consisting of $60,000 taxable interest income and $20,000 qualified dividend income. The trust distributes the full $80,000 equally to Beneficiary X ($40,000) and Beneficiary Y ($40,000). Beneficiary X has substantial capital losses from stock sales. How does the character of the DNI allocation affect each beneficiary differently, and why can''t Beneficiary X use capital losses to offset the trust distribution?',
  choices = '["Each beneficiary receives $40,000 characterized as $30,000 taxable interest and $10,000 qualified dividends (pro rata share of DNI character) — Beneficiary X cannot offset the distribution with capital losses because the income retains its character as interest and dividends from the trust, and capital losses can only offset capital gains, not ordinary income or qualified dividends", "Each beneficiary receives $40,000 of undifferentiated trust income that can be classified at their discretion, so Beneficiary X can designate the entire $40,000 as capital gain and offset it with capital losses", "The trustee can allocate all $60,000 of interest to Beneficiary X and all $20,000 of dividends to Beneficiary Y to optimize each beneficiary''s individual tax situation through a specific allocation provision", "Both beneficiaries report the $40,000 as ordinary income regardless of the underlying trust income character because trust distributions lose their original character and are taxed as ordinary income to the recipient"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 652(b), amounts included in a beneficiary''s gross income from a trust retain the same character as in the hands of the trust. The $80,000 DNI consists of 75% taxable interest and 25% qualified dividends. Each beneficiary''s $40,000 share carries the same proportions: $30,000 interest (taxed as ordinary income) and $10,000 qualified dividends (taxed at preferential rates). Beneficiary X''s capital losses cannot offset either component because under Section 1211, capital losses offset only capital gains (with up to $3,000 of excess deductible against ordinary income). The $30,000 of interest is ordinary income and the $10,000 of dividends, while taxed at capital gains rates, is not a capital gain for purposes of the capital loss offset rules. This character retention principle is fundamental to Subchapter J — the trust acts as a conduit, not a converter. (B) is wrong because beneficiaries cannot redesignate income character. (C) is wrong because absent specific trust provisions, allocation must be pro rata. (D) is wrong because distributions retain their character under Section 652(b).',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1009;

