-- NONTAXABLE DISPOSITIONS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1236: Nontaxable Dispositions — Analyzing installment method gross profit ratio and timing effects
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Kessler sells commercial land (adjusted basis $180,000) to an unrelated buyer for $500,000. The buyer pays $100,000 at closing and delivers a $400,000 installment note payable in four equal annual installments of $100,000 plus 6% interest. Separately, Bennett sells equipment (adjusted basis $90,000, original cost $150,000, $60,000 accumulated depreciation) for $200,000 on the same installment terms. How does the installment method under Section 453 treat these two sales differently in Year 1, and why must Bennett recognize more income upfront?',
  choices = '["Both sellers use the same gross profit ratio calculation and recognize income proportionally as payments are received — there is no difference in Year 1 treatment because the installment method applies uniformly to all asset types", "Kessler''s gross profit ratio is 64% ($320,000 gain / $500,000 price), so Year 1 recognition is $64,000 (64% x $100,000 payment). Bennett must first recapture $60,000 of depreciation as ordinary income in full in Year 1 under Section 453(i) regardless of payment received, then apply a separate gross profit ratio for the remaining $50,000 gain — this front-loads Bennett''s tax burden because depreciation recapture cannot be deferred", "Kessler recognizes $100,000 of gain in Year 1 (the entire payment received) because land gains are not eligible for installment treatment, while Bennett defers all gain under the installment method", "Both sellers recognize the same amount in Year 1 because the depreciation recapture rules were repealed by the TCJA and all gains are now treated as capital gains eligible for full installment deferral"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 453, the installment method allows gain to be recognized proportionally as payments are received, using the gross profit ratio (total gain / total contract price). For Kessler: gain is $320,000 ($500,000 - $180,000), gross profit ratio is 64%, and Year 1 recognition is $64,000 (64% x $100,000 down payment). However, Section 453(i) requires that depreciation recapture under Section 1245 be recognized in full in the year of sale regardless of payment schedule. Bennett has $60,000 of Section 1245 recapture ($60,000 accumulated depreciation) that must be recognized as ordinary income in Year 1, plus a portion of the remaining $50,000 capital gain based on the installment formula. This means Bennett recognizes at least $60,000 in Year 1 versus Kessler''s $64,000 — but the character is critically different (ordinary vs. capital). The policy rationale is that Congress did not want depreciation recapture to be deferred. (A) is wrong because Section 453(i) creates different treatment. (C) is wrong because land gains are eligible for installment treatment. (D) is wrong because depreciation recapture rules remain in effect.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1236;

-- ID 1237: Nontaxable Dispositions — Comparing involuntary conversion replacement standards
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Logistics owns two properties destroyed in a federally declared disaster in 2026. Property A is a warehouse (adjusted basis $400,000) for which Ashford receives $650,000 in insurance proceeds. Property B is a vacant investment lot (adjusted basis $200,000) condemned by the city for road expansion, for which Ashford receives $350,000 in condemnation proceeds. Ashford is considering replacement properties. How do the replacement requirements and time periods differ between the insurance-funded replacement and the condemnation, and what happens if Ashford replaces the warehouse with a retail building?',
  choices = '["Both properties have the same two-year replacement period and must be replaced with property that is ''similar or related in service or use'' — Ashford cannot replace the warehouse with a retail building because the use differs", "The warehouse (casualty) must be replaced within two years with property ''similar or related in service or use'' — meaning functionally similar — while the condemned lot (condemnation of real property) gets a three-year replacement period and only requires ''like-kind'' replacement under Section 1033(g), a broader standard. Replacing the warehouse with a retail building would likely fail the functional-use test because a warehouse and retail space serve different operational functions", "Both properties qualify for the three-year replacement period because they were destroyed in a federally declared disaster, and both only need to meet the ''like-kind'' standard because real property is always subject to the more lenient test", "The warehouse replacement period is four years because it was destroyed in a federally declared disaster area, and the condemned lot has a two-year period — both must meet the ''similar or related in service or use'' standard"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033, the replacement standards differ based on the nature of the conversion. For casualties and theft (the warehouse), the replacement property must be ''similar or related in service or use'' — the narrower functional-use test (also called the ''end-use'' test) that requires the replacement to serve the same function. A warehouse replaced with a retail building would likely fail because storage and retail are different functional uses. The replacement period is generally two years from the close of the first tax year in which gain is realized (note: for business/investment property in a federally declared disaster, Section 1033(h) may extend this). For condemnation of real property held for business or investment, Section 1033(g) applies the broader ''like-kind'' standard (similar to Section 1031) and provides a three-year replacement period. Under this standard, any real property held for business or investment can replace other real property. (A) is wrong because condemnation of real property uses the like-kind standard, not functional use. (C) is wrong because the disaster provision does not change the replacement standard. (D) partially confuses the disaster extension with the general rules.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1237;

