-- INDIVIDUAL TAX PLANNING: COMPENSATION (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 897: Compensation — Analyzing Section 83(b) election risk-reward tradeoff
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Technologies grants VP of Engineering Sarah Nakamura 10,000 shares of restricted stock on January 15, 2026, when FMV is $12 per share. The shares vest in three years. Sarah''s CPA advises her to consider a Section 83(b) election. By the vesting date, the stock could be worth $50 per share (bull case) or $5 per share (bear case). Assuming Sarah is in the 35% bracket at both grant and vesting, how does the Section 83(b) election affect her tax outcome under each scenario, and what is the irreversible risk?',
  choices = '["The Section 83(b) election is always beneficial because it locks in the lower $12 per share value for tax purposes — in the bull case she saves tax on $380,000 of appreciation, and in the bear case she can claim a $70,000 ordinary loss deduction for the decline in value", "With a Section 83(b) election, Sarah pays $42,000 tax at grant (35% x $120,000). In the bull case, the $380,000 of appreciation ($500,000 - $120,000) is taxed as long-term capital gain at vesting — a significant savings over 35% ordinary rates. In the bear case, the stock is worth only $50,000 at vesting, and Sarah has permanently overpaid by $24,500 ($42,000 tax paid minus $17,500 she would have owed without the election) — and if she forfeits the stock, the $42,000 is lost with no deduction allowed under Section 83(b)(1)", "The Section 83(b) election defers all taxation until sale, which is why it is always preferable to immediate recognition", "The Section 83(b) election converts all future appreciation to capital loss rather than capital gain, providing downside protection through the loss deduction"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 83(b), electing within 30 days of grant causes immediate recognition of the FMV ($120,000) as ordinary income, triggering $42,000 of tax. All subsequent appreciation is capital gain (long-term if held 1+ year from grant). In the bull case: without the election, Sarah would recognize $500,000 of ordinary income at vesting, paying $175,000 in tax. With the election, she pays $42,000 upfront plus capital gains tax on $380,000 (at 20% = $76,000), totaling $118,000 — a $57,000 savings. In the bear case: without the election, she would recognize $50,000 at vesting ($17,500 tax). With the election, she already paid $42,000 and cannot recover the overpayment. If the stock is forfeited before vesting, Section 83(b)(1) explicitly denies any deduction — the $42,000 is permanently lost. This irreversible risk is the critical planning consideration. (A) is wrong because no deduction is allowed on forfeiture. (C) is wrong because the election accelerates, not defers, taxation. (D) is wrong about capital loss conversion.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 897;

-- ID 898: Compensation — Comparing ISO vs NQSO tax treatment and AMT implications
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Software grants two executives stock options on the same date. Executive A receives 5,000 incentive stock options (ISOs) with an exercise price of $20 per share. Executive B receives 5,000 nonqualified stock options (NQSOs) with the same $20 exercise price. Both exercise when FMV is $60 per share and sell at $80 per share two years after exercise. How do the total tax consequences differ between the two executives, and why does the ISO create an AMT exposure that the NQSO avoids?',
  choices = '["Executive A (ISO) pays no tax at exercise and recognizes $300,000 of long-term capital gain at sale ($80 - $20 = $60 x 5,000), but must include $200,000 ($60 - $20 = $40 x 5,000) as an AMT preference item at exercise under Section 56(b)(3), potentially triggering AMT — Executive B (NQSO) recognizes $200,000 of ordinary income at exercise (spread of $40 x 5,000) and $100,000 of capital gain at sale, with no AMT adjustment because the spread was already taxed", "Both executives pay identical total tax because ISOs and NQSOs produce the same economic income — the only difference is timing, which washes out upon sale", "Executive A pays capital gains tax on the entire $300,000 with no AMT impact because the AMT preference for ISOs was repealed by the TCJA, while Executive B pays ordinary income tax on the full $300,000", "Executive A recognizes $200,000 of ordinary income at exercise (same as Executive B) plus $100,000 of capital gain at sale, because the ISO holding period requirement was not met"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 421(a), ISO exercises produce no regular tax recognition if the holding period requirements are met (held 2+ years from grant and 1+ year from exercise). Executive A''s entire $300,000 gain ($80 - $20 = $60 x 5,000) is long-term capital gain at sale. However, under Section 56(b)(3), the $200,000 spread at exercise ($60 - $20 = $40 x 5,000) is an AMT adjustment item, which may trigger alternative minimum tax in the exercise year — a significant cash flow risk if the stock price declines before sale. Executive B (NQSO) recognizes $200,000 as ordinary income at exercise under Section 83(a), with the employer receiving a corresponding deduction. B''s basis becomes $60 per share, so the subsequent sale at $80 produces $100,000 of capital gain. B has no AMT exposure because the spread was already included in regular taxable income. The ISO provides potentially lower total tax (all capital gains) but introduces AMT risk; the NQSO avoids AMT but front-loads ordinary income. (B) is wrong because rate differentials and AMT produce different outcomes. (C) is wrong because the AMT preference was not repealed. (D) is wrong because the holding period was met (2 years after exercise).',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 898;

