-- ENTITY TAX COMPLIANCE: NOLs AND CONSOLIDATED RETURNS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 952: NOLs and Consolidated Returns — Analyzing post-TCJA NOL limitation effect on tax planning
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Caldwell Manufacturing generates a $3,000,000 net operating loss in 2025. In 2026, the company has taxable income of $4,000,000 before the NOL deduction. The CFO asks whether the company can eliminate its entire 2026 tax liability using the NOL carryforward. How does the post-TCJA 80% limitation under Section 172(a)(2) affect Caldwell''s 2026 taxable income, and what is the strategic implication of the remaining taxable income that cannot be offset?',
  choices = '["Caldwell can offset the full $3,000,000 NOL against the $4,000,000 of income because the 80% limitation only applies to NOLs generated before 2018, not post-TCJA losses", "Caldwell''s NOL deduction is limited to $3,200,000 (80% of $4,000,000 taxable income), and since the carryforward is only $3,000,000, the full $3,000,000 is deductible — the 80% cap is not binding here because the NOL is less than 80% of taxable income, leaving $1,000,000 of taxable income and $210,000 in federal tax", "Caldwell can deduct only $2,400,000 (80% of $3,000,000 NOL) and must carry the remaining $600,000 forward indefinitely", "Caldwell''s NOL deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income, capping the deduction at $3,200,000 — but the actual NOL of $3,000,000 is fully usable because it falls below the cap, resulting in $1,000,000 of residual taxable income and demonstrating that post-TCJA, no amount of NOL carryforwards can reduce taxable income below 20% of the pre-NOL amount, fundamentally changing the planning landscape from pre-TCJA when NOLs could eliminate taxable income entirely"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under IRC Section 172(a)(2), as amended by the TCJA, post-2017 NOLs can offset only 80% of taxable income in any carryforward year (with indefinite carryforward but no carryback for most taxpayers). Caldwell''s 80% cap is $3,200,000 (80% x $4,000,000). Since the available NOL ($3,000,000) is less than the cap ($3,200,000), Caldwell uses the entire $3,000,000, leaving $1,000,000 taxable. The strategic implication is significant: even with massive NOL carryforwards, a corporation will always pay tax on at least 20% of its pre-NOL taxable income. This is a fundamental shift from pre-TCJA law, where NOLs could reduce taxable income to zero. Companies must now incorporate this ''minimum taxable income floor'' into cash flow projections and estimated tax payments. (A) is wrong because the 80% limitation applies to post-2017 NOLs, not pre-2018 NOLs. (B) reaches the correct numbers but frames the analysis less completely. (C) is wrong because the 80% applies to taxable income, not the NOL amount itself.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 952;

-- ID 954: NOLs and Consolidated Returns — Analyzing consolidated return affiliation threshold requirements
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Graystone Holdings is evaluating its corporate structure for consolidated return eligibility. Graystone owns 85% of the voting stock and 90% of the total value of Subsidiary A (a domestic C corporation), 75% of the voting stock of Subsidiary B (a domestic C corporation), and 100% of Subsidiary C (a foreign corporation). How does the Section 1504(a) affiliation test apply to each subsidiary, and what is the effect of excluding Subsidiary B and C on Graystone''s consolidated tax position?',
  choices = '["All three subsidiaries are included in the affiliated group because Graystone owns more than 50% of each, which meets the affiliation threshold under Section 1504(a)", "Subsidiary A qualifies (85% voting power and 90% value both exceed the 80% thresholds), Subsidiary B fails (75% voting power is below the 80% threshold), and Subsidiary C is excluded as a foreign corporation under Section 1504(b)(3) — the exclusion of B means its income and losses are reported separately, preventing Graystone from offsetting B''s losses against A''s income, and C''s exclusion means its income is addressed through the Subpart F/GILTI regime rather than consolidation", "Subsidiary A and B both qualify because the 80% test is based on the average of voting stock and value, and B''s average of 75% and assumed value ownership meets this threshold, while only Subsidiary C is excluded", "All domestic subsidiaries automatically qualify for consolidation when the parent owns any amount of stock, and only foreign subsidiaries are excluded under Section 1504(b)(3)"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1504(a)(2), an affiliated group requires the common parent to own at least 80% of both the total voting power and 80% of the total value of each includible corporation''s stock. Subsidiary A''s 85% voting and 90% value both exceed 80% — it qualifies. Subsidiary B''s 75% voting power fails the 80% voting threshold, even though value ownership may exceed 80% — both tests must be met independently. Subsidiary C is excluded under Section 1504(b)(3), which removes foreign corporations from the definition of ''includible corporation.'' The practical consequences are substantial: Graystone cannot offset Subsidiary A''s profits against Subsidiary B''s losses (B must file separately), losing the primary benefit of consolidation — income-loss netting among members. Subsidiary C''s foreign income enters Graystone''s return through different mechanisms (Sections 951, 951A, 245A) rather than line-by-line consolidation. (A) is wrong because the threshold is 80%, not 50%. (C) is wrong because voting and value are independent tests, not averaged. (D) is wrong because the 80% ownership threshold must be met.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 954;

COMMIT;
